  R"LPROFILE({"schema":"loom.runtime_profile/1","domain":"chat_context","revision":1,"description":"Order of context classes in the chat prompt and the selector profiles. Orders: legacy reproduces the historical order byte for byte (system, memory, graph, project, history); stable_first puts the slowly changing classes first so provider prompt prefixes stay reusable (system, memory, project, history, graph); stability (the default preset since M1-D2) adds the classes profile (the stable part of what the app knows about the user) and rules (the rules about the user) right after the system instruction: system, profile, rules, memory, project, history, graph. The active-task block and the question always come last. Since work item M1-K1a the kernel fills them: rules is the standing rules about the person (under the orders without this class they stay in the instruction part, as before), profile is what the person told about themselves, rendered by profile_prefix. Both are empty when there is nothing to send, and then stability sends the same bytes as stable_first. Orders are data: a user can add their own named order. selector_profiles are the presets of the context selector (a view kind of the same name): each is a bundle of knobs that already exist, not a new engine. In a profile a null number or name means: what the setting says now (graph_memory_max_nodes, graph_memory_depth, chat_representation, chat_flow); a source set to false is not read by that profile; a number of a source that is false is 0 and ignored; history_window null = the whole conversation; budget_tokens null = no limit of its own. profile_prefix is the recipe of the class profile: a fixed header, a fixed line format, the subgraphs of the node You (profile.subgraphs in user.pack, sorted by order_for_prefix) and which of them (and which keys) each profile_detail_level carries. Presets, not rules.","defaults":{"context_order":"stability","orders":{"legacy":["system","memory","graph","project","history"],"stable_first":["system","memory","project","history","graph"],"stability":["system","profile","rules","memory","project","history","graph"]},"selector_profiles":[{"id":"loom","label":{"pl":"Loom (preset)","en":"Loom (preset)"},"why":{"pl":"Zwykły czat z pełnym kontekstem: instrukcja, to, co wiem o Tobie, reguły o Tobie, pamięć, projekt, rozmowa i powiązania z grafu. Początek żądania jest taki sam w każdej turze, więc dostawca może go zapamiętać i bywa taniej.","en":"A normal chat with the full context: the instruction, what I know about you, the rules about you, memory, project, the conversation and the links from the graph. The start of the request is the same in every turn, so the provider can remember it and it can be cheaper."},"order":"stability","sources":{"profile":true,"rules":true,"memory":true,"project":true,"history":true,"graph":true,"search":false,"kb":false},"breadth":{"nodes":null,"search_limit":0,"history_window":null},"depth":{"hops":null},"representation":null,"flow":null,"budget_tokens":null,"status":"preset","note":{"pl":"Liczba węzłów i głębokość grafu, sposób wysłania rozmowy i przepływ są takie, jak w ustawieniach (preset: 20 węzłów, głębokość 2). Jądro stosuje ten profil: porządek „stability”, źródła i liczby z tabeli (tu: jak w ustawieniach) oraz sposób wysłania rozmowy. Część „Profil” zawiera tylko to, co sam powiedziałeś o sobie w wywiadzie; bez wywiadu jest pusta i żądanie jest takie jak w porządku „stable_first”.","en":"The number of graph nodes, the depth, how the conversation is sent and the flow are as in the settings (preset: 20 nodes, depth 2). The kernel applies this profile: the order “stability”, the sources and the numbers of the table (here: as in the settings) and how the conversation is sent. The part “Profile” holds only what you said about yourself in the interview; without an interview it is empty and the request is the same as with the order “stable_first”."}},{"id":"loom_legacy","label":{"pl":"Loom (dawny)","en":"Loom (legacy)"},")LPROFILE",
